  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nAfter nearly hitting 100F at the El Paso Airport on both Friday\nand Saturday we are finally seeing a pattern change to not as hot\n(too early to call it cooler), with a good chance for rain.\nCurrently the upper level ridge that has kept us dry and hot this\nSummer has moved off and is centered over the southeast U.S. The\ncirculation around the high will pull up moisture from northern\nMexico. This increased moisture will help fuel scattered afternoon\nand evening thunderstorms this evening through much of the coming\nweek. Our high temperatures each day will be steadily falling so\nthat by Thursday and Friday we will have high temperatures near or\n(gasp) a little below average. Seasonal temperatures look to\ncontinue into the coming weekend. These lower high temperatures\nwill give the lowlands highs in the 80's for Thursday into the\nweekend, which we have not seen consistently since early June. \n\nThe increased moisture will give us better rain chances, but it\nwill also increase the risk for flash flooding. Precipitable water\nvalues (PW's) will increase into the range of 1.1 to 1.3 which is\nwell above average for this time of year. For now the main threat\nfor flooding will be in the Gila Region on Tuesday, but it may \nexpand to include locations west of the Rio Grande on Wednesday \nand across the whole area on Thursday. The chances for rain \ncontinue into Saturday. The forecast beyond Saturday is a little \nmore in doubt as the extended GFS dries us out, while the extended\nECMWF continues the influx and moisture and rain chances.",
